ok today we're learning about energy  stores so there's 8 different ways that  energy can be stored and we're going to  learn about each one now energy can be  transferred between the different stores  but today we're just focused on the  stores themselves so the first one we're  going to focus on is the kinetic energy  stores so whenever we think about  kinetic energy the word key word we're  thinking about is movement so if this  person runs faster this person has more  energy in the kinetic energy store the  second type of energy is gravitational  potential energy so any object in a  gravitational field that has the  potential to fall has gravitational  potential energy so if I lift this rock  up further from the Earth's surface it  has more gravitational potential energy  as I lift this pen up closer towards the  camera  I'm giving it more gravitational  potential energy next up its chemical  energy so chemical energy is any type of  energy which sorry anything which can  release energy by a chemical reaction  now there's two main examples that we  need to think about or would need to  know the two examples we need to know is  food and fuel now these are meant to be  little lumps of coal right here so  whenever we have food or fuel that's  type of chemical energy because it's  energy stored within these that's  released when a chemical reaction is  occurring next up is electrostatic  energy now this electrostatic energy is  when we have charges so if we have  opposite charges they're going to  attract if we have two charges with the  same for example both positive they're  going to repel so attract means come  together repel means go apart an example  could be someone who has rubbed their  hair a head against a balloon giving  both the balloon and the hair opposite  charges and then it causes them to  attract like this okay next up is  magnetic  now magnetic energy I guess is kind of  similar in a way  - electrostatic in the way that we have  attraction and repulsion so if I just  quickly draw these poles of the magnets  on here the top one because the poles  are opposite so we have self next to  north and north next to South because  these are opposite these are going to  attract whereas the bottom on here  because they are the same pole next to  each other these are going to repel so  that's what magnetic energy is all about  next up we have elastic potential so I'm  going to draw a little example right  here which is of a slingshot so as I  pull this slingshot back I'm giving this  object more elastic potential energy  more energy in the elastic potential  store so whenever we're dealing with  stretching of a material we're putting  more energy within the elastic potential  store okay next up is nuclear energy  now nuclear energy is energy which is  stored within the nucleus or in the  nucleus of an atom so just as a little  recap here's my nucleus remember  electrons go around the outside so it's  energy which is stored within the  nucleus which could be released when we  have nuclear reactions for example in a  nuclear power station number eight and  the final one is thermal energy so  thermal energy I'm just going to do a  little diagram here to show a  thermometer so Fermin energy is  basically when we're thinking about Heat  so so when an object is hotter it has  more energy in the thermal store so  those are the eight different types of  energy but there's just a little way  that I've come up with to help everyone  remember them which is kg cement so  imagine a kilogram of cement so  this stands for each of the different  energy stores so we have kinetic  gravitational potential energy chemical  electrostatic magnetic  elastic potential nuclear and thermal so  we just need to remember this kilogram  of cement kg cements and those are your  eight different energy stores thank you
 

